<p> --Help Supply	    2,428    2,403    2,404    2,265</p>
<p> -Government	     19,568   19,587   19,596   19,394</p>
<p>Household Survey-Civilian Employment Data, Seasonally Adj.</p>
<p>(Number, 1,000s):  March  February--(Prev)  January--(Prev)</p>
<p> Workforce	  136,319  135,634  135,634  135,848  135,848</p>
<p> -Employed	  129,175  128,430  128,430  128,580  127,580</p>
<p> -Unemployed	  7,144    7,205    7,205    7,268    7,268</p>
<p> --Job Leavers	  784	787	787	890	890</p>
<p>--Job Leavers as</p>
<p> Pct of Unemployed   11.0     10.9     10.9     12.3     12.3</p>
<p>Wall Street economists expected March non-farm payrolls to rise by 196,000 and a jobless rate of 5.2 percent. They also had forecast a 0.4 percent rise in March average hourly earnings and the workweek to average 34.8 hours.</p>
<p>The department said that over the past year average hourly earnings increased by 4.0 percent, the largest 12-month gain since the 12 months ended June 1990.</p>
<p>The non-farm payroll data are based on a survey of employers, and the jobless rate and other civilian employment data are based on a survey of households.</p>
